{
  "id": "L14-exact-total",
  "level": 14,
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "use": "practice",
  "status": "ready",
  "order": 1,
  "title": "Exact Total Stretches",
  "statement": "A shop records its daily profit (which may be negative). Return how many stretches of consecutive days add up exactly to k.",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "[1, 2, 3], 3",
      "output": "2",
      "explanation": "[1, 2] and [3]."
    },
    {
      "input": "[1, -1, 1, -1], 0",
      "output": "4"
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "0 <= profits.length <= 2 * 10^4",
    "-1000 <= profits[i] <= 1000"
  ],
  "answerKey": {
    "pattern": "Running (prefix) sums with a hash map of how often each sum appeared",
    "keyPoints": [
      "Keep the running total of profits",
      "A stretch ending today sums to k when an earlier running total equals running - k",
      "Add how many times running - k has appeared, using a map from running total to count",
      "Start the map with 0 seen once"
    ],
    "timeComplexity": "O(n)",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(n)",
    "edgeCases": [
      "Negative values (a sliding window does not work)",
      "The map must start with {0: 1} for stretches that start on day one",
      "k = 0"
    ],
    "acceptableSlowerApproaches": [
      "Try every start and end with a running sum: O(n^2)"
    ]
  },
  "hints": [
    "Checking every start and end is O(n^2). What does the sum of days i to j equal in terms of running totals?",
    "sum(i..j) = running(j) - running(i - 1). You need earlier running totals equal to running(j) - k.",
    "Count earlier running totals in a map, and start it with 0 seen once."
  ],
  "starterCode": {
    "javascript": "function exactTotals(profits, k) {\n  // return how many stretches sum to k\n}",
    "java": "class Solution {\n  int exactTotals(int[] profits, int k) {\n    // return how many stretches sum to k\n  }\n}"
  },
